  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Phase 2 Youth employment for resilience building</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia has one of the youngest populations on earth, but youth unemployment rates are high and increasing over the past period. Specifically for youth of pastoral areas, the limited opportunities for income and livelihood have a negative influence on the coping capacity towards the impact of disaster. Cordaid and its local partners Hundee and EECMY-DASSC propose &#x201c;Youth employment for resilience building&#x201d; project in Bale zone, Madda Walabu district. The aim is to enhance the productive capacity of the youth through addressing three key areas:  Skill development that is foreseen to improve both soft and hard skill gaps of youth,  Facilitate access to market-based solutions including access to finance  Create a conducive system for youth employment through fostering collaboration of different government departments in helping youth employability.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project target group are young people of age 18-35 and are actively looking for a job and unable to be involved in some type of job or engaged in jobs lower to their skill level. Youth with a low income/asset background will also be targeted to explore their potential and resources in their environment to make use for startups. Women and men will have equal chance to join the project considering the already burdened labour share among the women.  Both married and unmarried male and female have equal possibility to benefit from the project. The direct beneficiaries of the project are 1600 of which,  1200 are vulnerable and unemployed youths and  400 are women and these women households will be organized into 20 VSLAs</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To contribute to sustained transformation of youth from unemployment towards being productive citizens and inspirations for change through behavioral interventions, skills development and facilitating access to market-based solutions in Mada Walabu and West Wolabu districts .</narrative>
  </description>
